WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.919
In 1935, Albert Einstein was famously&nbsp;
disturbed by an idea in quantum physics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.199
When two particles are quantum entangled,

00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:19.386
they can instantaneously interact across vast distances.

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:24.000
Einstein found&nbsp;
this phenomenon "spooky."

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:25.923
Just the next year,

00:00:25.923 --> 00:00:30.443
Alan Turing identified a&nbsp;
problem computers would never be able to solve.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.034
Computers usually operate based on inputs and&nbsp;
outputs,

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:38.359
but sometimes they can get stuck in infinite loops.

00:00:38.359 --> 00:00:41.200
Turing proved there's&nbsp;
no way to tell when this will happen,

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.977
and he called it the halting problem.

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:47.667
Today we&nbsp;
recognize it in the Spinning Wheel of Death.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.620
In the 1930s, quantum entanglement and the&nbsp;
halting problem seemed to have nothing to&nbsp;do with each other.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.521
But this year, they&nbsp;
combined in a landmark proof

00:00:56.521 --> 00:01:03.531
that set off a cascade of solutions to open problems in&nbsp;
computer science, physics and mathematics.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.267
This is Henry Yuen.

00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:07.178
He's one of five&nbsp;
co-authors of the proof.

00:01:07.178 --> 00:01:11.027
Yuen: You know, what this paper is about, it's in computational&nbsp;
complexity theory,

00:01:11.027 --> 00:01:13.924
which is like a branch of theoretical computer science.

00:01:13.924 --> 00:01:17.176
And it talks&nbsp;
about the computational

00:01:17.176 --> 00:01:21.672
power of a model of what's called interactive proofs.

00:01:21.672 --> 00:01:28.672
An interactive proof&nbsp;
is a kind of logical interrogation method that models computation as the exchange of messages&nbsp;
between two parties

00:01:28.672 --> 00:01:31.096
-- a prover, and a verifier.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.210
To understand how this works,

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:38.670
imagine the verifier&nbsp;
is a police officer interrogating two subjects: The provers.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:42.675
You can't go out and confirm&nbsp;
every single detail of the suspect's stories,

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.720
but by asking the right questions and&nbsp;
pitting your subjects against each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.340
you can catch them in a lie

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.564
or develop confidence&nbsp;
that the facts check out.

00:01:51.564 --> 00:01:55.093
Yuen: The policemen will place these two suspects in different rooms,

00:01:55.093 --> 00:02:03.961
but it just so happens that these suspects also can share quantum entanglement to coordinate&nbsp;
their responses in some spooky quantum mechanical fashion.

00:02:03.961 --> 00:02:08.438
Yuen: The policeman's job is to try to&nbsp;
figure out what the truth really is.

00:02:08.438 --> 00:02:13.931
The main result of this paper is that even though the these&nbsp;
suspects

00:02:13.931 --> 00:02:18.130
might share quantum entanglement, the policemen can actually

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:24.444
interrogate them in such a&nbsp;
way that the policemen can figure out the truth of

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.375
any mathematical statement corresponding to

00:02:27.375 --> 00:02:30.867
an&nbsp;
enormously complicated range of questions.

00:02:30.867 --> 00:02:38.021
This means that, in theory, a super powerful quantum&nbsp;
computer could verify answers to even unsolvable problems like Turing's halting problem.

00:02:38.021 --> 00:02:41.683
Yuen:&nbsp;
It involves all these really beautiful pieces from different areas.

00:02:41.683 --> 00:02:46.568
Things from computer&nbsp;
science, things from mathematics and physics,

00:02:46.568 --> 00:02:50.532
that you know before we didn't think were that&nbsp;
related to each other, and yet they are.

00:02:50.532 --> 00:02:53.266
I think it points to something much more interesting. I&nbsp;
don't know what,

00:02:53.266 --> 00:02:55.982
but you know there's a feeling that there's something more to,

00:02:55.982 --> 00:03:00.464
you know,&nbsp;
there's like a whole new world to discover.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.065
This is John Horton Conway.

00:03:05.065 --> 00:03:09.096
His infamous knot&nbsp;
problem eluded mathematicians for half a century.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:14.480
The question asked whether the Conway knot was&nbsp;
actually a slice of a higher dimensional knot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.489
a property called sliceness.

00:03:16.489 --> 00:03:23.498
This question&nbsp;
proved answerable for thousands of similar knots, but Conway's resisted every attempt to untangle&nbsp;
it.

00:03:23.498 --> 00:03:28.105
Lisa Piccirillo was a graduate student when she first heard about the Conway knot.

00:03:28.105 --> 00:03:30.683
Piccirillo:&nbsp;
Well I just thought it was completely ridiculous

00:03:30.683 --> 00:03:33.000
that we didn't know whether this knot was slice or&nbsp;
not.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.587
We have a lot of tools for doing this sort of thing, so i didn't understand like, why

00:03:36.587 --> 00:03:38.880
for some&nbsp;
11-crossing-knot this should be so difficult.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:45.354
I think the next day, which was a Sunday, I&nbsp;
just started trying to run the approach

00:03:45.354 --> 00:03:48.909
for fun and I worked on it a bit in the evenings just&nbsp;
to try to see

00:03:48.909 --> 00:03:52.172
what's supposed to be so hard about&nbsp;this problem.

00:03:52.172 --> 00:03:56.012
And then the following week, I had a&nbsp;
meeting with Cameron Gordon,

00:03:56.012 --> 00:03:58.848
a senior topologist, in my department

00:03:58.848 --> 00:04:01.869
about something else, and I&nbsp;
mentioned it to him there.

00:04:01.869 --> 00:04:07.668
He was like, "Oh really? You showed the Conway knot's not slice?"&nbsp;
Like, show me.

00:04:07.668 --> 00:04:14.561
And then I started to put it up and he started asking kind of detailed questions, and&nbsp;
then

00:04:14.561 --> 00:04:17.906
at some point he got, he got very excited.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:22.704
Piccirillo's proof was published in&nbsp;
the Annals of Mathematics.

00:04:22.867 --> 00:04:24.596
Piccirillo: It was it was quite surprising to me.

00:04:24.596 --> 00:04:26.276
I mean, it's just one knot.

00:04:26.276 --> 00:04:27.412
In general,

00:04:27.412 --> 00:04:31.535
when mathematicians prove things, we like to prove&nbsp;
really broad, general statements:

00:04:31.535 --> 00:04:36.432
All objects like this have some property. And I proved like,&nbsp;
one knot has a thing.

00:04:36.432 --> 00:04:38.082
I don't care about knots.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:44.882
So, I do care about three and four dimensional&nbsp;
spaces, though.

00:04:44.882 --> 00:04:47.509
And it turns out that,

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:53.597
when you want to study three and four dimensional&nbsp;
spaces, you find yourself studying knots anyway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:01.107
Mathematics can sometimes seem like a jumbled&nbsp;
mosaic.

00:05:01.107 --> 00:05:04.418
Major areas of study have never been fully written down,

00:05:04.418 --> 00:05:10.223
and doing so would have required&nbsp;
using thousands of other definitions that don't yet exist.

00:05:10.223 --> 00:05:16.905
Now, imagine you had a Library of&nbsp;
Alexandria that contained the entire history and the sum total of mathematical knowledge.

00:05:16.905 --> 00:05:22.107
With everything catalogued, you could program an AI to verify increasingly complex proofs,

00:05:22.107 --> 00:05:25.449
and one&nbsp;
day, hopefully, come up with new ones on its own.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.234
At Imperial College London, Kevin Buzzard is in&nbsp;
the process of digitizing math.

00:05:30.234 --> 00:05:36.338
He's teaching it to a software called Lean, which draws upon&nbsp;
an ever-growing library of proofs and theorems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.338 --> 00:05:41.459
Buzzard: I decided that this software was very&nbsp;
interesting about three years ago,

00:05:41.459 --> 00:05:44.476
and have since made a huge amount of noise about it.

00:05:44.476 --> 00:05:48.442
We took&nbsp;
some very, very modern mathematics and just

00:05:48.442 --> 00:05:51.911
show, you know, we taught it to Lean and Lean could&nbsp;
handle it.

00:05:51.911 --> 00:05:55.860
And basically, it was at that point that I realized it should be able to do anything,&nbsp;
really.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:59.551
Lean has a big maths library, 450,000

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:00.944
lines of code long.

00:06:00.944 --> 00:06:03.574
The library just grows all the&nbsp;
time every day, you know,

00:06:03.574 --> 00:06:07.123
10 more, 10 more pull requests get added to this library.

00:06:07.123 --> 00:06:09.146
The growth&nbsp;
is immense.

00:06:09.146 --> 00:06:12.439
It's just slowly eating mathematics.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:15.222
Buzzard: Computers speak a certain language,

00:06:15.222 --> 00:06:18.549
so there's a&nbsp;
fast-moving language which you have to learn.

00:06:18.549 --> 00:06:22.029
And then once you've done that, you just explain&nbsp;
the mathematics but in that language.

00:06:22.029 --> 00:06:25.556
And the big problem is that in maths departments&nbsp;
across the world,

00:06:25.556 --> 00:06:27.670
we're teaching people the mathematical ideas,

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:31.831
but nobody's teaching&nbsp;
them the language that these computers speak.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:36.288
Buzzard: Mathematics is not quite what it's&nbsp;
sold.

00:06:36.288 --> 00:06:40.870
I mean, we tell the undergraduates that mathematics is this completely rigorous,

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:43.346
you know, theory built from the axioms.

00:06:43.346 --> 00:06:46.859
And in practice, that's not how mathematics&nbsp;
is done.

00:06:46.859 --> 00:06:48.451
Computers are quite picky,

00:06:48.451 --> 00:06:51.667
because they do want to know what's going on.

00:06:51.667 --> 00:06:56.249
So one&nbsp;
challenge we have faced, is that people are slightly imprecise,

00:06:56.249 --> 00:06:57.663
and computers don't buy it.

00:06:57.663 --> 00:07:03.388
Before you can teach something to the computer, you do need to understand it perfectly.

00:07:03.388 --> 00:07:09.251
The&nbsp;
act of engaging with the details can sometimes clarify the situation.

00:07:09.251 --> 00:07:12.204
You end up with a proof&nbsp;
which is slightly messy, and then you try and

00:07:12.204 --> 00:07:16.275
type it into a computer and at the end of it,&nbsp;
you end up with a with a slicker argument.

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:21.520
One of the goals is that we want to see&nbsp;
an entire undergraduate curriculum in it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:26.400
And you know, give us another couple of years,&nbsp;
and then we will be able to honestly say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.919
you know, that it's as smart as&nbsp;
an undergraduate in some sense.

